WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.760
Welcome. Today we ask one simple question. Is there enough room for all the wires on a chip?

00:06.720 --> 00:12.800
Imagine the chip is a city. Every wire is a car, and each tile of the grid is one city block.

00:13.840 --> 00:19.840
Start with one block. This road has two lanes. Two lanes is its capacity.

00:20.800 --> 00:28.240
Wires arrive like cars. Car 1 fits. Car 2 fits. Now the road is full.

00:29.200 --> 00:36.160
Car 3 has no lane. Demand is 3, capacity is 2, so 3 minus 2 is equal to plus 1.

00:37.120 --> 00:42.160
That is a traffic jam. Real chips are cut into a grid of tiles called G cells.

00:43.120 --> 00:48.640
Each one keeps two numbers, demand and capacity. This tile holds cells A to F.

00:49.600 --> 00:55.040
Its demand is 3.5 but its capacity is only 2.0, so it is oversubscribed.

00:56.000 --> 01:01.040
Picture each tile as a bucket. Capacity is the line the water must not cross.

01:02.080 --> 01:10.800
Demand 1.0, plenty of room. Demand 2.0, full. Demand 3.2, it spills over.

01:11.760 --> 01:16.480
Surplus equals demand minus capacity. Zero or below is safe.

01:17.520 --> 01:21.200
Above zero means overflow. Now check the whole chip.

01:22.160 --> 01:27.120
We visit every tile and work out its surplus. Most tiles stay common blue.

01:28.160 --> 01:33.520
One tile turns yellow, 3.5 minus 2.0 is equal to plus 1.5.

01:34.480 --> 01:37.760
Before you trust a heat map, list the oversubscribed tiles.

01:38.800 --> 01:42.320
Here there is just one. What if the road gets narrower?

01:43.280 --> 01:47.680
Capacity is a knob, so let's turn it from 2.0 down to 1.0.

01:48.640 --> 01:54.080
Same wires, same tiles. Watch the borderline tiles turn yellow one by one.

01:55.120 --> 02:00.720
Lower capacity means more traffic jams. That is why capacity is part of the contract.

02:01.680 --> 02:10.880
Your turn. Capacity is 2.0. Demands are 1.4, 2.0, 2.6 and 3.0.

02:11.840 --> 02:15.440
How many are oversubscribed? Think for 3 seconds.

02:16.400 --> 02:23.200
2. 2.6 and 3.0 are over. 2.0 is full, not over.

02:24.160 --> 02:29.440
Encoded is just three lines. Work out the surplus, keep tiles above zero, count them.

02:30.400 --> 02:35.680
Notice the strict greater than. A tile that is exactly full is not oversubscribed.

02:36.640 --> 02:41.520
Watch out for three beginner traps. 1. Compare wires with wires.

02:42.480 --> 02:48.480
Write your units down. 2. A zero tile beside hot tiles is not automatically safe.

02:49.520 --> 02:52.640
3. Reset the starter placement when you change capacity.

02:53.600 --> 02:56.240
Great job. You can compute surplus-

